## Key Points

- **Precondition:** The array must be **sorted** in ascending order. Binary search will not work correctly on unsorted data.
- **Time complexity:** O(log n) — the search space halves with each iteration.
- **Space complexity:** O(1) for iterative, O(log n) for recursive (due to call stack).
- **Overflow safety:** Always use `left + (right - left) // 2` instead of `(left + right) // 2` to avoid integer overflow in languages with fixed-width integers (relevant for Java, less so for Python's arbitrary-precision ints).
- **Edge cases:** Empty array, single-element array, target smaller than all elements, target larger than all elements, duplicate values (binary search typically returns any matching index, not necessarily the first).

### Variants

- **Finding first/last occurrence:** When duplicates exist, modify the condition to keep searching left or right after a match.
- **Closest element:** Track the closest element seen when the target is not found.
- **Rotated array:** A modified binary search can handle arrays that were sorted then rotated at an unknown pivot.
